   plot="Allen argues that the pleasure of beauty is the pleasure of an easy and healthy exercise of the nervous system: that the beautiful is what the eye and the brain can take in without strain, and that the arts must therefore be understood as the deliberate production of that physiological state.",
   ctx="1877: the new physiology of Helmholtz and Grant Allen's own science teaching, the aesthetics of Ruskin and Pater, and the Victorian attempt to found a science of the mind.",
   sig="Physiological Aesthetics is the founding work of evolutionary aesthetics in English and one of the first scientific accounts of aesthetic experience: it anticipated the modern psychology of art by a century, it influenced Vernon Lee and the whole tradition of physiological criticism, and it is a landmark of Victorian scientific thought.",
