   plot="The essays record the experience of particular places — Tuscan hill towns, Venetian squares, English gardens, the Roman campagna — and argue that a place has a character, a 'genius loci', which the sensitive visitor can feel and which the arts of the past have helped to create.",
   ctx="1893-99: the aesthetic travel essay, the English cult of Italy, the new psychology of place and atmosphere, and the Victorian love of the picturesque.",
